---
### Projects
[p1] - You will have {{color=project|five projects}}. Each project must be completed on your own, but you may talk about any concepts you want.
...
!anim p1 color #808080 0.5s
[p2] - Projects will be due at {{color=project|Midnight}} at the end of their due date. You may, on occasion have up to a 5 day extension, but you should let us know before the deadline.
...
!anim p2 color #808080 0.5s
[p3] - You must {{color=project|check in}} a version of your code that passes at least 25% of test cases by one week before the due date.
...
!anim p3 color #808080 0.5s
[p4] - A valid {{color=project|Makefile}} must be submitted with your project.
---
### Tentative Projects
**{{color=#0097ebbf|Project 1:}}** Lexical analysis.  You will build an interpreter for **Marquee**, emphasizing the tokenization of inputs.
**{{color=#0097ebbf|Project 2:}}** Parsing and Abstract Syntax Tree construction.
**{{color=#0097ebbf|Project 3:}}** Semantic Analysis and generating WebAssembly output.
**{{color=#0097ebbf|Project 4:}}** Memory Management including implementation of strings and arrays.
**{{color=#0097ebbf|Project 5:}}** A high-performance language with Advanced Language Features will emphasize Code Analysis and Optimizations.
---
### Academic Honesty
...
- **{{color=emp|Stay in charge}}** of your own project; don't lose your agency.
...
- Do not **{{color=warning|outsource}}** the entire project to an AI or another person.
...
- Always {{color=emp|attribute credit}} for code implemented by another person or AI.
...
- You must {{color=emp|understand all code}} that you submit for a project.
...
- {{color=warning|Do not provide false information}} to the instructor about course matters.
...
- {{color=warning|Do not facilitate other students}} in any of these activities.
...
- You must be able to {{color=emp|explain how your code works}}!
...
- Violation of these rules will result in a {{color=warning|final grade of **0.0**}}.

---
### Why should you care?
...
[p1] - Compilers draw together all of the theory and techniques that you’ve learned about in most of your previous computer science courses.
...
!anim p1 color #808080 0.5s
[p2] - You will learn how to write a simple compiler to solve the kinds of problems you may face in a career as a programmer.
...
!anim p2 color #808080 0.5s
[p3] - You will gain a deeper understanding of how compilers work and be able to write better code.
...
!anim p3 color #808080 0.5s
[p4] - You will learn techniques to write other useful tools, such as parsers, interpreters, and debuggers.
...
!anim p4 color #808080 0.5s
[s10_154] {thick=1, textcolor=#dc1f05, size=40} !rect (108, 580) (1062, 80) Warning : It will be a **LOT** of programming!
!anim s10_154 enter grow 0.8s
