---
### How are languages implemented?
...
- Strategies depend on many factors, including how much **{{color=keyword|pre-processing}}** must be done before the program can run, and how {{color=keyword|**CPU specific**}} the executable can be.
...
- {{color=emp|Interpreters}} run a program "as is" with little or no-preprocessing, but no changes need to be made to run on a different platform
...
- {{color=emp|Compilers}} take time to do extensive pre-processing, but will generally run a program 2 to 20 times faster
---
### Language Implementations
...
- Many languages use a combination of compiler and interpreter to get the many benefits of each.
...
- {{color=keyword|Java}} and {{color=keyword|Microsoft’s .NET}} are hybrid languages that compile into virtual/intermediate assembly languages (Java bytecode, CIL), which can then be interpreted on any computer.
...
- Other languages ({{color=keyword|BASIC}}, {{color=keyword|Lisp}}) have both compilers and interpreters available.
...
- Many modern interpreters (e.g., {{color=keyword|JavaScript, PyPy}}) perform “just in time” compilation to convert routines into optimized native code when they are first used.
---
### History of Compiler Development
...
- In 1953 IBM developed the 701 EDPM{{br}}(Electronic Data Processing Machine),{{br}}its first general purpose computer,{{br}}built as a "defense calculator" in the{{br}}Korean War.
@(695, 160) {w=560px} ![image](https://www.ibm.com/content/dam/connectedassets-adobe-cms/worldwide-content/arc/cf/ul/g/b5/41/701_205-copy.jpg/_jcr_content/renditions/cq5dam.web.3840.3840.jpeg)
...
- No high-level languages were available,{{br}}so all programming was done in{{br}}assembly language.
---
### History of Compilers
...
- As expensive as these early computers were, most of the money companies spent was for software development, due to the complexities of assembly language programming.
...
- In 1953, John Backus came up with the idea of{{br}}"speed coding", and developed the first interpreter.{{br}}Unfortunately, this was 10 to 20 times slower than{{br}}programs written in assembly.{{br}}{{br}}He was sure he could do better...
@(900, 270) {w=280px} ![image](https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b2/John_Backus_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled)
@(680, 640) {size=32px, color=name} **John Backus**
---
### History of Compilers
...
- In 1954, Backus and his team released a research paper titled "*{{color=name|Preliminary Report, Specifications for the IBM Mathematical FORmula TRANslating System, FORTRAN.}}*"
...
- FORTRAN I was released in 1956, totaling 25,000 lines of assembly code. Compiled programs ran almost as fast as handwritten assembly!
...
- Projects that had previously taken two weeks to write now took only two hours.
...
- By 1958 more than half of all software was written in FORTRAN.
---
### Modern Compilers
...
- Compiler theory has not changed a great deal since the days of Backus. They still consist of two main components:
...
- The {{color=emp|front-end}} reads in the program in the source languages, makes sense of it, and stores it in an internal representation…
...
- …and the {{color=emp|back-end}} converts the internal representation into the target language, perhaps with optimizations. The target language is typically an assembly language, but it is often easier to use a more established, higher-level language.
